REFERENCE   11 (residues 1 to 119)
  AUTHORS   Jost,A. and Pfeifer,F.
  TITLE     Interaction of the gas vesicle proteins GvpA, GvpC, GvpN, and GvpO
            of Halobacterium salinarum
  JOURNAL   Front Microbiol 13, 971917 (2022)
   PUBMED   35966690
  REMARK    FUNCTION, SUBUNIT, AND SUBCELLULAR LOCATION.;
            STRAIN=PHH1; PLASMID=pHH1
            Publication Status: Online-Only
COMMENT     On or before Sep 27, 2005 this sequence version replaced
            gi:3183026, gi:7484030.
            [FUNCTION] A minor component of the gas vesicle, also found in
            soluble extracts (PubMed:21158390). May play a role in
            transcription and/or RNA stability and in GV assembly (Probable)
            (PubMed:35966690, PubMed:8606186). Gas vesicles are hollow, gas
            filled proteinaceous nanostructures found in several microbial
            planktonic microorganisms. They allow positioning of halobacteria
            at the optimal depth for growth in the poorly aerated, shallow
            brine pools of their habitat (PubMed:33711860).
            {ECO:0000269|PubMed:21158390, ECO:0000269|PubMed:33711860,
            ECO:0000305|PubMed:35966690, ECO:0000305|PubMed:8606186}.
            [FUNCTION] Expression of a 9.5 kb p-vac DNA fragment containing 2
            divergently transcribed regions
            (gvpD-gvpE-gvpF-gvpG-gvpH-gvpI-gvpJ-gvpK-gvpL-gvpM and
            gvpA-gvpC-gvpN-gvpO) allows H.volcanii to produce gas vesicles
            (PubMed:10894744, PubMed:1404376, PubMed:7651141). A minimal gas
            vesicle can be made in H.volcanii by gvpA1-gvpO1
            gvpF1-gvpG1-gvpJ1-gvpK1-gvpL1-gvpM1; lack of enough GvpJ1 prevents
            formation (PubMed:10894744). The same region restores gas vesicle
            production in H.halobium without the p-vac locus, but it still has
            the c-vac locus (PubMed:1398080). {ECO:0000269|PubMed:10894744,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:1398080, ECO:0000269|PubMed:1404376,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:7651141}.
            [SUBUNIT] Forms homodimers, forms a GvpN1-GvpO1 heterodimer,
            interacts with GvpC1 (via the latter's C-terminus), GvpF1, GvpI1
            and GvpL1, might interact with GvpA1.
            {ECO:0000269|PubMed:35966690}.
            [SUBCELLULAR LOCATION] Gas vesicle {ECO:0000269|PubMed:21158390}.
            Cytoplasm {ECO:0000305|PubMed:21158390}. Note=Probably on the
            exterior surface of the gas vesicle. {ECO:0000305|PubMed:1404376}.
            [INDUCTION] Maximally transcribed in late log phase, probably part
            of a gvpC1-gvpO1 operon (Probable). Has several transcripts with
            and without other genes in the locus, expressed from early- to
            late-log but not in stationary phase (PubMed:8606186). Gas vesicles
            appear earlier when grown in static culture, possibly due to
            O(2)-limitation (PubMed:33711860). {ECO:0000269|PubMed:33711860,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:8606186, ECO:0000305|PubMed:7651141}.
            [DISRUPTION PHENOTYPE] Gas vesicle formation is wild-type (strain
            NRC-1), expressed in situ (PubMed:8002589). No gas vesicle
            formation, strongly decreased accumulation of gvpACN transcripts
            (strain PHH1), expressed in H.volcanii (PubMed:10894744,
            PubMed:8606186). {ECO:0000269|PubMed:10894744,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:8002589, ECO:0000269|PubMed:8606186}.
            [MISCELLANEOUS] Encoded in a 14-gene plasmid locus called p-vac
            which produces predominantly short, spindle-shaped gas vesicles
            during all stages of growth. {ECO:0000269|PubMed:1404376,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:33711860}.
            [SIMILARITY] Belongs to the gas vesicle GvpO family. {ECO:0000305}.
